WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.880
Now designing the data architecture.

00:01.880 --> 00:04.320
It is exactly like building a house.

00:04.320 --> 00:10.120
So before construction starts, an architect is gonna go and design a plan, a blueprint for the house,

00:10.120 --> 00:15.240
how the rooms will be connected, how to make the house functional, safe and wonderful.

00:15.240 --> 00:20.640
And without this blueprint from the architects, the builders might create something unstable, inefficient,

00:20.640 --> 00:22.080
or maybe unlivable.

00:22.080 --> 00:23.800
The same goes for data projects.

00:23.840 --> 00:26.040
A data architect is like a house architect.

00:26.080 --> 00:30.280
They design how your data will flow, integrate and be accessed.

00:30.280 --> 00:35.720
So as data architects, we make sure that the data warehouse is not only functioning, but also scalable

00:35.720 --> 00:36.920
and easy to maintain.

00:36.920 --> 00:38.840
And this is exactly what we will do now.

00:38.880 --> 00:44.720
We will play the role of the data architect, and we will start brainstorming and designing the architecture

00:44.720 --> 00:45.880
of the data warehouse.

00:45.880 --> 00:50.400
So now I'm going to show you a sketch in order to understand what are the different approaches in order

00:50.400 --> 00:52.280
to design a data architecture.

00:52.280 --> 00:57.760
And this phase of the project usually is very exciting for me because this is my main role in data projects.

00:57.760 --> 01:03.160
I am a data architect, and I discuss a lot of different projects where we try to find out the best

01:03.160 --> 01:04.520
design for the projects.

01:04.560 --> 01:05.710
All right, so now let's go.

01:05.750 --> 01:10.950
Now, the first step of building a data architecture is to make a very important decision to choose

01:10.950 --> 01:12.710
between four major types.

01:12.710 --> 01:15.550
The first approach is to build a data warehouse.

01:15.550 --> 01:18.430
It is very suitable if you have only structured data.

01:18.430 --> 01:23.750
And your business want to build solid foundations for reporting and business intelligence.

01:23.750 --> 01:26.630
And another approach is to build a data lake.

01:26.670 --> 01:31.590
This one is way more flexible than a data warehouse, where you can store not only structured data,

01:31.590 --> 01:34.590
but as well semi and unstructured data.

01:34.590 --> 01:40.790
We usually use this approach if we have mixed types of data like database tables, logs, images, videos

01:40.790 --> 01:46.830
and your business want to focus not only on reporting but as well on advanced analytics or machine learning.

01:46.830 --> 01:50.270
But it's not that organized like a data warehouse and data lakes.

01:50.270 --> 01:56.390
If it's too much unorganized and turns into data swamp and this is where we need the next approach.

01:56.390 --> 01:59.590
So the next one we can go and build data Lake house.

01:59.630 --> 02:02.870
So it is like a mix between data warehouse and Data Lake.

02:02.910 --> 02:07.990
You get the flexibility of having different types of data from the data lake, but you still want to

02:08.030 --> 02:11.110
structure and organize your data like we do in the data warehouse.

02:11.110 --> 02:13.230
So you mix those two words into one.

02:13.230 --> 02:16.470
And this is a very modern way on how to build that architecture.

02:16.470 --> 02:19.870
And this is currently my favorite way of building data management system.

02:19.870 --> 02:23.590
Now, the last and very recent approach is to build a data mesh.

02:23.590 --> 02:24.910
So this is a little bit different.

02:24.910 --> 02:31.150
Instead of having centralized data management system, the idea now in the data mesh is to make it decentralized.

02:31.150 --> 02:36.190
You cannot have like one centralized data management system because always if you say centralized then

02:36.190 --> 02:37.390
it means bottleneck.

02:37.390 --> 02:42.870
So instead you have multiple departments and multiple domains where each one of them is building a data

02:42.870 --> 02:44.830
product and sharing it with others.

02:44.830 --> 02:47.150
So now you have to go and pick one of those approaches.

02:47.150 --> 02:50.390
And in this project we will be focusing on the data warehouse.

02:50.390 --> 02:53.190
So now the question is how to build the data warehouse.

02:53.190 --> 02:56.590
Well there is as well four different approaches on how to build it.

02:56.590 --> 02:58.870
The first one is the Inmon approach.

02:58.870 --> 03:03.870
So again you have your sources and the first layer you start with the staging where the raw data is

03:03.870 --> 03:04.310
landing.

03:04.310 --> 03:10.150
And then the next layer you organize your data in something called enterprise data warehouse where you

03:10.150 --> 03:14.020
go and model the data using the third normal format.

03:14.060 --> 03:17.100
It's about like how to structure and normalize your tables.

03:17.100 --> 03:21.220
So you are building a new integrated data model from the multiple sources.

03:21.260 --> 03:22.660
And then we go to the third layer.

03:22.660 --> 03:27.740
It's called the data marts where you go and take like small subset of the data warehouse.

03:27.740 --> 03:31.940
And you design it in a way that is ready to be consumed from reporting.

03:31.940 --> 03:37.580
And it focuses on only one topic, like for example, the customer sales or products.

03:37.580 --> 03:42.300
And after that you go and connect your BI tool like power BI or Tableau to the data marts.

03:42.340 --> 03:46.180
So with that you have three layers to prepare the data before reporting.

03:46.220 --> 03:47.300
Now moving on to the next one.

03:47.300 --> 03:48.940
We have the simple approach.

03:48.940 --> 03:49.980
He says, you know what?

03:50.020 --> 03:54.060
Building this enterprise data warehouse, it is wasting a lot of time.

03:54.060 --> 03:59.340
So what we can do, we can jump immediately from the stage layer to the final data marts.

03:59.380 --> 04:04.660
Because building this enterprise data warehouse, it is a big struggle and usually wastes a lot of time.

04:04.660 --> 04:09.300
So he always wants you to focus on building the data marts quickly as possible.

04:09.300 --> 04:11.820
So it is faster approach than inmon.

04:11.820 --> 04:16.020
But with the time you might get chaos in the data marts because you are not always focusing on the big

04:16.020 --> 04:16.460
picture.

04:16.570 --> 04:21.530
And you might be repeating same transformations and integrations in different data marts.

04:21.530 --> 04:26.170
So there is like trade off between the speed and consistent data warehouse.

04:26.170 --> 04:28.930
Now moving on to the third approach, we have the data vaults.

04:28.930 --> 04:31.330
So we still have the stage and the data marts.

04:31.330 --> 04:35.250
But it says we still need this central data warehouse in the middle.

04:35.250 --> 04:39.010
But this middle layer we're going to bring more standards and rules.

04:39.010 --> 04:44.810
So it tells you to split this middle layer into two layers, the row vault and the business vaults.

04:44.810 --> 04:49.690
In the row vault you have the original data, but in the business vault you have all the business rules

04:49.690 --> 04:53.170
and transformations that prepares the data for the data marts.

04:53.210 --> 04:59.170
So Data Vault, it is very similar to the Inmon, but it brings more standards and rules to the middle

04:59.170 --> 04:59.690
layer.

04:59.730 --> 05:04.610
Now I'm going to go and add a fourth one that I'm going to call it Medallion Architecture.

05:04.610 --> 05:09.490
And this one is my favorite one because it is very easy to understand and to build.

05:09.490 --> 05:13.650
So it says you're going to go and build three layers bronze, silver and gold.

05:13.650 --> 05:18.250
The bronze layer, it is very similar to the stage, but we have understood with the time that the stage

05:18.250 --> 05:24.850
layer is very important because having the original data as it is, it helps a lot by traceability and

05:24.850 --> 05:25.690
finding issues.

05:25.730 --> 05:27.730
Then the next layer we have the silver layer.

05:27.730 --> 05:31.930
It is where we do transformations, data cleansing, but we don't apply yet.

05:31.970 --> 05:33.090
Any business rules.

05:33.130 --> 05:35.090
Now moving on to the last layer the gold layer.

05:35.090 --> 05:37.370
It is as well very similar to the data marts.

05:37.370 --> 05:43.130
But there we can build different types of objects not only for reporting, but as well for machine learning,

05:43.130 --> 05:46.010
for AI and for many different purposes.

05:46.010 --> 05:50.770
So they are like business ready objects that you want to share as a data product.

05:50.770 --> 05:55.530
So those are the four approaches that you can use in order to build a data warehouse.

05:55.650 --> 06:00.930
So again, if you are building a data architecture you have to specify which approach you want to follow.

06:00.930 --> 06:03.250
So at the start we said we want to build a data warehouse.

06:03.250 --> 06:07.130
And then we have to decide between those four approaches and how to build a data warehouse.

06:07.130 --> 06:10.730
And in this project we will be using the medallion architecture.

06:10.730 --> 06:16.050
So this is a very important question that you have to answer as the first step of building a data architecture.

06:16.090 --> 06:16.490
All right.

06:16.490 --> 06:18.650
So with that you have decided on the approach.

06:18.650 --> 06:20.970
So we can go and mark it as done.

06:20.970 --> 06:25.130
The next step we are going to go and design the layers of the data warehouse.
